WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
So I want to finalize our conversation about&nbsp;
Donald Trump getting impeached a second time, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.800
we've been filming, the House has started to move&nbsp;
forward with what I described earlier, an initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.880
proposal for the 25th Amendment to be invoked.
Every indication that that is not going to happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.600
and that impeachment will be subsequently the&nbsp;
path forward. We also spoke earlier about how some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.720
Republican officials now say that it's time for&nbsp;
Trump to go even that he should be impeached. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.000
talked about Pat Toomey saying Trump should leave.&nbsp;
We talked about Chris Christie saying that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.160
Trump's inciting of the violent insurrection is&nbsp;
certainly impeachable. But where is the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.120
on this? That's the next question. And we do have&nbsp;
new polling on that. According to a new Reuters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:49.600
Ipsos poll, 57 percent of Americans want Donald&nbsp;
Trump to be immediately removed from office. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.800
is sort of like a do not pass go, do not collect&nbsp;
200 dollars type of thing. And it is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:02.400
directly related to his encouragement of the&nbsp;
protests last week, which escalated into a deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:09.200
riot inside the U.S. Capitol building. Most of the&nbsp;
people who want Trump removed are still Democrats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:17.360
Republicans remain much more supportive of Donald&nbsp;
Trump, and seven out of 10 of those who voted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:24.720
Trump in November oppose his removal. But again,&nbsp;
when it's only 70 percent of Republicans who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.480
against the removal, you end up with a situation&nbsp;
where, led by Democrats, with many independents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.040
and some Republicans, the majority of the country,&nbsp;
it's not disapproves of the job Trump is doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:44.320
It's believes that Donald Trump should be removed.&nbsp;
So there's a few different aspects to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.600
one. Yes, it's notable that so many people&nbsp;
in the country want the president removed.&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.160
But what's the other side of that? It's the&nbsp;
same as what we've been saying when we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.400
looked at Donald Trump's approval over&nbsp;
the last four years. How is it this low?&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.920
In other words, it should be dramatically, more&nbsp;
than slightly more than half of Americans wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:12.960
the president removed. The exact number&nbsp;
right now is 57 percent. When you blend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.920
Democrats, independents and Republicans, you&nbsp;
end up with about 57 percent of Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.800
wanting Trump to leave office. What about the&nbsp;
rest of them? And it's disappointingly low.&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.080
And I think it's important to remember, you&nbsp;
know, we've talked today about this idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.520
impeachment could be divisive or whatever the case&nbsp;
and how I think those are really bad arguments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.680
I think there's another aspect to this that's&nbsp;
important to to broach or to bring up. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.000
impeachment is not about punishment.&nbsp;
Impeachment is what you do when the facts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.520
say that it's warranted and if the facts&nbsp;
say that it's warranted, it must be done.&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:02.320
And one of the reasons I've seen some be hesitant&nbsp;
to support impeachment is they don't believe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.360
would be a sufficient punishment because Trump&nbsp;
is leaving anyway. I think that that's both not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.000
the point and also irrelevant because there is&nbsp;
a mechanism through which the Senate can vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.400
if the House and the Senate can vote to convict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.680
but they can also vote to strip Trump of the&nbsp;
ability to run in the future. And that requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.960
only a simple majority. And there are more things&nbsp;
at stake. There is Trump's million dollar a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.977 --> 00:03:32.880
presidential travel budget. There are&nbsp;
other things that are at play here. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.560
for the folks saying the impeachment wouldn't be&nbsp;
a strict enough punt, it wouldn't really be much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:45.040
of a punishment for Trump at this point. No.
One, I disagree in that being impeached and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.880
particularly if he was convicted or stripped of&nbsp;
the ability to run again would be humiliating for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.720
Donald Trump. And that's a it's quite a punishment&nbsp;
when you understand Donald Trump's psyche. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.560
are also other things that would be significantly&nbsp;
deleterious to Donald Trump's future. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:08.560
it is not about punishment. Trump's mishandling&nbsp;
of coronavirus is impeachable. Trump's inciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.480
of this riot is impeachable. And if he did&nbsp;
something impeachable, he must be impeached. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.320
I'm tired of it would be divisive or it wouldn't&nbsp;
be much of a punishment mindset. I'm also, by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.280
way, tired of the mindset that we can just keep&nbsp;
him under control for the next however many days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.160
It's not about that at this point in time. And&nbsp;
it's not true. No one has been able to keep him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.840
under control for any period of time. I mean, look&nbsp;
at look at even last week he incited the riot.&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.760
Then he was essentially, from what we&nbsp;
know, forced to go out there and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.200
oh, the violence was bad and there&nbsp;
will be a transition like, oh, wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.840
he sounds slightly presidential. It was&nbsp;
obviously disingenuous. And within hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.600
he was back on Twitter repeating the&nbsp;
same failed, the same debunked notions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:03.200
about voter fraud and 75 million votes and all&nbsp;
of this other stuff so nobody can control him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.760
It's not about that. It's not about punishment.&nbsp;
It's just what has to happen. And more than half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.550
of the country at this point believes that Donald&nbsp;
Trump should be immediately removed from power.

